# ============================ ACCOUNTS 5 ============================
LESSONS_A5 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The tax accounts: custody money", "11", "<p>This module covers the tax layer of ZhiftERP: the tax accounts and templates, VAT on both sides, withholding tax in both directions, the payroll deduction accounts, the monthly filing routine, the compliance calendar and the tax reserve, and handling authority queries.</p><p><b>The custody idea.</b> Most tax money in a trading business is money the business <b>holds</b> for someone else, not money it owes from its own pocket:</p><p>• The VAT charged on every Sales Invoice was collected from customers for the state.<br>• The WHT deducted from a supplier's payment is the supplier's tax, held in transit.<br>• PAYE is the employees' income tax, withheld at source.</p><p>This is visible in the postings: all three are credits to liability accounts — module 1's ₦7,500 VAT line. The working consequence runs through the whole module: <b>custody money is not revenue, not cash flow, and not available.</b> A business that spends its VAT balance as working capital is spending someone else's money, and remittance day becomes a monthly crisis. Chapter 7's reserve procedure is the fix.</p><p><b>The accounts to create.</b> Each custody is a leaf with <b>Account Type = Tax</b>:</p><p>• <b>VAT Payable</b> — output custody — and <b>Input VAT</b> — the claimable asset that offsets it (Chapter 3).<br>• <b>WHT Payable</b> — custody when deducting from suppliers — and <b>WHT Receivable</b> — the mirror asset: the business's own tax prepaid through customers' deductions (Chapter 4).<br>• <b>The payroll deduction accounts</b> — PAYE Payable, Pension Payable, and the levy family (Chapter 5).<br>• Separately, the business's own tax — the Companies Income Tax provision — which is a cost, not custody. Keep the distinction: withheld-for-others is custody; owed-from-profits is the business's own expense.</p><p><b>One pattern, five appearances.</b> Every custody follows the same cycle: an account, fed automatically by documents and templates, reconciled monthly to a <b>register report</b>, cleared by a remittance <b>Payment Entry</b>, and verified back at zero. Learn the cycle once in Chapter 6 and apply it to each tax.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Nigerian frame. Chapter 3: VAT — templates, item tax, and the register bridges. Chapter 4: WHT — the Tax Withholding Category and the certificate chase. Chapter 5: PAYE and the payroll accounts. Chapter 6: the six-step filing routine. Chapter 7: the calendar and the reserve. Chapter 8: authority queries and audits. Chapter 9: the HealthTrade case. Chapter 10: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Nigerian frame", "11", "<p>The system machinery is universal; the obligations are Nigerian. This chapter is the map. Current rates and thresholds are the accountant's living knowledge — this manual teaches which machinery serves each obligation, not the numbers.</p><p><b>The taxes that touch a trading business.</b></p><p>• <b>VAT</b> — the consumption tax on supplies, collected on sales and remitted monthly, federal. Configured through the tax templates (Chapter 3).<br>• <b>WHT</b> — withholding on specified payment types, in both directions. Configured through Tax Withholding Categories (Chapter 4).<br>• <b>PAYE</b> — employees' income tax, withheld monthly, remitted <b>to the state of each employee's residence</b>. This split is worth memorising early: VAT and Companies Income Tax run federal; PAYE runs to states. One business, several authorities, one calendar.<br>• <b>Companies Income Tax (CIT)</b> — the business's own annual tax on profits, provisioned through the year; the accountant's territory.<br>• <b>The payroll statutory family</b> — pension, housing, insurance, and training levies (Chapter 5), applicability confirmed by headcount and sector.</p><p><b>The 2025 reform — what changed in practice.</b> The Nigeria Tax Act 2025 and its companion acts consolidated the statute book and restructured administration. Three working consequences:</p><p>1. <b>The authority landscape was reorganised</b>, with registrations and filing rails updated. The accountant confirms the business's current registrations against the new structure.<br>2. <b>Thresholds and reliefs were reshaped</b> — small-company regimes, rate structures, exemption bands. The accountant re-reads eligibility annually; the system's job is to be reconfigurable: template rates adjustable, item categories re-flaggable, without disturbing history.<br>3. <b>Compliance moved further digital</b> — e-filing as the norm, and e-invoicing advancing. In ZhiftERP, FIRS e-invoicing runs through the <b>NGE Portal</b> integration, which transmits invoices to the authority's rails. The consequence that matters for this module: the authority increasingly reconciles your filed returns against your transmitted invoices <b>automatically</b>. A business whose returns are prepared from the system's registers matches by construction; one that files from a parallel spreadsheet gets the mismatch letters.</p><p><b>The boundary.</b> This module teaches the machinery: accounts, templates, registers, routines, evidence. Tax <b>positions</b> — what qualifies, what rate applies, what relief is claimable — belong to the accountant and the tax adviser. Chapter 8 shows the boundary at its most important moment: the audit.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — VAT: templates, item tax, and the bridges", "13", "<p>VAT setup and the monthly position, end to end.</p><p><b>Output side — the sales templates.</b> Open <b>Sales Taxes and Charges Template</b>. The standard template carries one row: Type = On Net Total, Account Head = VAT Payable, Rate = the current VAT rate. Set it as default; every Sales Invoice picks it up and posts the VAT to the custody account automatically. The <b>Sales Register</b> report (Accounting &gt; Sales Register) is the document-level mirror: every invoice with its net and its tax, the return's raw material.</p><p><b>Input side — the purchase templates.</b> The <b>Purchase Taxes and Charges Template</b> mirrors it: Account Head = Input VAT, an asset. The claim's evidence is the supplier's proper invoice — which is why invoice quality is a money question: a ₦100 price with claimable input beats ₦99 without. The <b>Purchase Register</b> report mirrors the sales side.</p><p><b>Item-level rates — the mixed-supply setup.</b> Some items do not carry the standard rate. Configure this at the item, once:</p><p>1. Create <b>Item Tax Templates</b> for the non-standard treatments — e.g. <i>VAT Zero Rated</i> (rate 0 against VAT Payable) and <i>VAT Exempt</i>.<br>2. On each affected <b>Item record</b>, add the Item Tax Template in the Tax section. From then on, every invoice line for that item applies its own rate over the document template.<br>3. The distinction matters: <b>zero-rated</b> supplies still appear in the return and preserve input claims on their costs; <b>exempt</b> supplies fall outside the charge, and input VAT attributable to them is generally not claimable — the apportionment is the accountant's computation, fed by registers that can tell the streams apart.</p><p>A pharmacy whose basic-medicine lines ride an exemption while its cosmetics shelf is standard-rated gets this right <b>at the item master</b> — after which every sale classifies itself. Get it wrong there and every return inherits the error.</p><p><b>The monthly position.</b> Output charged minus allowable input equals the position: <b>payable</b> in a normal trading month (remit the net), or a <b>carry-forward credit</b> in a heavy-buying month (the credit offsets future output; carry-forward is the practical road).</p><p><b>The two bridges — the pre-filing check.</b> Before any return is filed:</p><p>1. Sales Register tax total for the month = VAT Payable account movement for the month (General Ledger report, filtered to the account).<br>2. Purchase Register tax total = Input VAT account movement.</p><p>Both to the kobo. A divergence found now is a bookkeeping task; found after filing, it is an amended return. With the authority reconciling transmitted invoices automatically (Chapter 2), the bridges are not good practice — they are survival.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — WHT: the Tax Withholding Category, both directions", "13", "<p>Withholding tax runs in both directions, and each direction has its setup.</p><p><b>As agent — deducting from suppliers.</b> The system automates this through the <b>Tax Withholding Category</b> doctype:</p><p>1. Create a category per deduction type the business meets — e.g. <i>WHT Professional Services</i> — with its rate, threshold, and the account: <b>WHT Payable</b>. The types and current rates come from the accountant's table.<br>2. On each affected <b>Supplier record</b>, set the Tax Withholding Category.<br>3. On the supplier's Purchase Invoice or Payment Entry, tick <b>Apply Tax Withholding Amount</b> (it defaults on once the supplier carries a category). The system computes the deduction and posts it: the ₦1,000,000 professional invoice settles as ₦950,000 to the supplier and ₦50,000 credited to WHT Payable — the supplier's tax, held.</p><p>The agent's two follow-through duties:</p><p>• <b>Remit monthly</b> by the calendar (Chapter 6's routine; the account zeroes after).<br>• <b>Deliver certificates.</b> The certificate is the supplier's evidence for their own tax credit; an agent who deducts and never delivers certificates injures every supplier it pays. The deduction detail — who, what, when, how much — comes from the General Ledger report on WHT Payable grouped by party, or the withholding detail report; one listing serves both the remittance schedule and the certificate run.</p><p><b>As sufferer — customers deducting from you.</b> Corporate and government customers deduct WHT from their payments: the ₦1,000,000 receivable settles as ₦950,000 cash plus a ₦50,000 deduction. The deduction is <b>not</b> a discount or a mystery short-payment — it is the business's own income tax, prepaid. Record it in the receiving <b>Payment Entry's Deductions table</b>: one row, account = <b>WHT Receivable</b>, amount = the deduction — so the invoice settles in full and the asset builds transaction by transaction.</p><p>Two rules make the asset real:</p><p>1. <b>Capture at allocation.</b> Every short-payment identified as WHT is coded to the Deductions row then, not investigated months later.<br>2. <b>Chase the certificates.</b> Keep the standing worklist: deductions taken, certificates outstanding, per customer, aged — worked like any receivable. An uncertificated deduction is money in limbo: suffered but unprovable.</p><p><b>Where the asset goes.</b> WHT suffered offsets the business's CIT at filing — the credit the accountant applies, evidenced by the certificates. A business that never chases certificates pays its own tax twice, quietly, every year. Chapter 9 prices this at HealthTrade.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — PAYE and the payroll deduction accounts", "12", "<p>The monthly payroll splits each employee's gross into destinations, and each destination is an account with an authority behind it. The payroll run itself belongs to the ZhiftHR/Payroll track; this chapter is the accounts view.</p><p><b>The accounts.</b></p><p>• <b>PAYE Payable</b> — the employees' income tax, withheld under the current bands and reliefs (the 2025 reform reshaped them; the accountant keeps the table, and the payroll configuration applies whatever the current structure is). Remitted monthly <b>to the state of each employee's residence</b>: a Lagos office with Ogun-resident staff files in both states, so the payroll register must split by state.<br>• <b>Pension Payable</b> — employee and employer portions, remitted to each employee's chosen fund administrator. Several PFAs, one schedule: the register splits by PFA. This is the tightest window in the family — remittance is pinned to <b>days from paying salaries</b>, not to the month's end.<br>• <b>The levy accounts</b> — housing fund, insurance trust, industrial training — where headcount and sector make them applicable; the accountant confirms.</p><p>Hold the distinction from Chapter 1: amounts <b>withheld from the employee</b> (PAYE, employee pension) are custody; amounts <b>borne by the employer</b> (employer pension share, the levies) are the business's own expense. The salary posting credits the custody accounts and debits expense for the employer-borne lines; where the payroll module generates the posting, read it once against this map.</p><p><b>The payroll tie — a monthly two-minute check.</b></p><p>1. The payroll register's column totals: gross, PAYE, pension by portion, net pay.<br>2. The salary posting's lines match those totals.<br>3. The custody accounts' movements (General Ledger report) match the posting.<br>4. After remittance, the custody accounts return to zero.</p><p>One chain. When it holds, every payroll question is answerable in minutes: the employee disputing a deduction (the register row and the payslip), the state querying a remittance (the schedule and the receipt), the auditor sampling a month (the chain walked).</p><p><b>The payslip.</b> Every employee receives a monthly payslip — gross, deductions itemised, net. It is the employee's version of the WHT certificate and the business's first defence in any deduction dispute. Not optional, not annual.</p><p><b>The annual layer.</b> Employer PAYE returns file in the new year's first month. Where the payroll register lived in the system all year, the return is an extract; where salaries lived in a spreadsheet with a monthly lump journal, it is a reconstruction project — Chapter 10's Pattern 5.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The filing routine and the zeroing check", "13", "<p>Every custody clears through the same monthly routine. Six steps, in order, per tax.</p><p><b>Step 1 — close the register.</b> The month's document-level detail, cut off at the period: the Sales and Purchase Registers for VAT, the withholding detail for WHT, the payroll register for PAYE. The register is the return's evidence and the account's mirror.</p><p><b>Step 2 — reconcile register to account.</b> The bridge (Chapter 3's procedure): register total against the account's movement in the General Ledger report, to the kobo. Work any divergence <b>now</b> — before filing it is a bookkeeping task; after filing it is an amended return, an authority conversation, or both.</p><p><b>Step 3 — prepare the return from the system.</b> The return restates the ledger. Do not compute it in a parallel spreadsheet: two computations guarantee a divergence, and the filed number wins the argument with the authority while the booked number wins it with the auditor — one source, or two problems. The spreadsheet's only lawful job is formatting the register extract into the filing layout.</p><p><b>Step 4 — file by the date.</b> The calendar's line (Chapter 7), owned by a named person.</p><p><b>Step 5 — remit.</b> One <b>Payment Entry</b> (Pay) from the tax-reserve bank account against the custody account, with the filing reference in Reference No and the return attached.</p><p><b>Step 6 — verify the zero.</b> After a clean cycle, each custody account returns to its explainable level: zero for fully remitted custodies, the stated carry-forward for VAT credit months. Check it in the Trial Balance or the account's General Ledger. A custody account that will not zero is telling one of four stories:</p><p>1. A register-account divergence — the bridge was skipped.<br>2. A remittance short or posted to the wrong account.<br>3. A prior month's residue compounding.<br>4. Custody spent — Chapter 1's disease, surfacing as arithmetic.</p><p>The zero check costs one glance and catches all four.</p><p><b>The evidence file.</b> Each cycle files its bundle: the register extract, the bridge reconciliation, the return as filed, the filing acknowledgment, the remittance Payment Entry, the zero verification. <b>One folder per tax per month</b> — attach the documents to the remittance entry and mirror them in the folder. This is Chapter 8's audit posture built monthly as a by-product; its absence is why authority queries paralyse unprepared businesses — the answer exists, but assembling it takes a week, and a week's silence reads badly.</p><p>Practical rule: steps 2 and 6 — the bridge and the zero — are the two that keep the routine honest, and the two that businesses under pressure skip first. Do not skip them.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The compliance calendar and the tax reserve", "12", "<p>The routine needs its dates and its money.</p><p><b>The calendar.</b> Build it once, with the accountant confirming the statutory days. The monthly shape:</p><p>• <b>VAT</b> — return and remittance in the month's third week, for the prior month.<br>• <b>WHT</b> — remittance on its earlier track; state-collected streams on their own days.<br>• <b>PAYE</b> — to each relevant state by its mid-month day.<br>• <b>Pension</b> — within days of paying salaries; the tightest window, pinned to the wage payment.</p><p>The annual layer above it: CIT filing on the business's horizon months after year-end; employer PAYE returns in January; the levy cycles. Record every obligation with its authority, day, <b>owner by name</b>, and evidence-folder location — on the policy page, reviewed annually and at every legal change. Set system <b>Notifications</b> or recurring ToDos against the dates so no line depends on memory.</p><p><b>The tax reserve.</b> Chapter 1 named the disease — custody spent. The fix is structural: segregate the custody <b>in cash</b>, not just in accounts.</p><p>1. Open a dedicated bank account — the <b>tax reserve</b> — with its Bank Account record and typed leaf (module 3), its purpose written on the record.<br>2. Sweep the custody cash into it on a rhythm, by <b>Internal Transfer</b>: the month's VAT position at close (or as it accrues), WHT when withheld, the payroll custodies at the payroll run.<br>3. The rule: <b>the reserve is not working capital.</b> Remittance day then becomes a transfer from a funded account, executed by the calendar — no scramble, no bridging loan, no conversation about which supplier waits so the state can be paid.</p><p><b>The honest answer to \"we need that float\".</b> Businesses under cash strain resist the sweep. The answer is not soft: the custody money was never available, so the strain is real and must be solved with real tools — collections acceleration (module 4), stock discipline, supplier terms, or actual credit at actual credit prices. Penalties and interest on unremitted custody are the most expensive credit line in Nigeria, taken by default. Chapter 9 shows this exact conversation at HealthTrade and the alternative that worked.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> One calendar, every obligation with an owner and a reminder; one reserve account, fed by scheduled Internal Transfers; remittances as funded transfers; and the penalty line watched to zero — it is the single clearest measure of whether this module is installed.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Authority queries and audits", "12", "<p>Sooner or later the authority asks questions. The preparation is a posture built monthly, not a scramble.</p><p><b>The posture in one sentence.</b> Registers tied to accounts tied to documents, filed on time, evidenced in folders. When that chain exists, every authority question is an extraction exercise; when it does not, every question is a reconstruction project — and reconstruction under deadline is where businesses concede assessments they never owed. The posture is Chapter 6's evidence file compounding: twelve folders per tax per year, each self-contained.</p><p><b>The three encounter types.</b></p><p>• <b>The desk review.</b> The authority's routine reconciliation of your filings against their records — increasingly automated as the e-invoicing rails mature: they hold your transmitted invoices and compare them to your returns themselves. A business whose returns came from its registers matches by construction; the mismatch letters go to the parallel-spreadsheet filers.<br>• <b>The query letter.</b> Specific questions on specific periods. Answer <b>on record</b>: in writing, evidence attached, within the stated window, factually, answering what was asked — completely, and nothing more. The accountant reviews every reply before it goes.<br>• <b>The field audit.</b> The authority in the building. Preparation is the standing posture plus logistics: a room, and a <b>liaison</b> — one named person through whom every request flows, so the audit receives consistent, logged responses instead of hallway answers. The role division is exact: the system's job is to <b>produce evidence</b> — registers extracted, drill-downs demonstrated, folders handed over — while <b>positions</b> are argued by the accountant and the tax adviser, never improvised at the table.</p><p><b>The common findings, pre-empted.</b> What trading-business tax audits actually find, each one a chapter of this module skipped:</p><p>1. Output VAT on invoices absent from returns — the bridge skipped (Chapter 3).<br>2. Input VAT claimed on defective supplier invoices — the evidence rule ignored (Chapter 3).<br>3. WHT deducted but remitted late or not at all — custody spent (Chapters 4, 7).<br>4. PAYE state splits improvised — the register that could not split (Chapter 5).<br>5. Payroll postings that tie to no register (Chapter 5).</p><p>A business that runs Chapters 3-7 has pre-empted the list, which is the point of running them.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Build the folders monthly; answer queries on record and inside the window; route field audits through one liaison; produce evidence and leave positions to the professionals. A clean posture turns the audit from a threat into an appointment.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Case study: HealthTrade's compliance year", "13", "<p>The tax layer, installed and run. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The setup and the first clean cycle (Chapters 1, 3, 6).</b> The four custody accounts are confirmed and typed. The item masters are audited for tax categories — the pharmacy's exempt basic-medicine lines against its standard-rated cosmetics: <b>14 items re-categorised</b> with Item Tax Templates, after which the Sales and Purchase Registers can tell the streams apart and the apportionment goes to the accountant with clean data. Then the first full monthly VAT routine: registers closed, both bridges tied to the kobo, the return prepared from the register extract, filed, remitted from the new reserve by Payment Entry, VAT Payable verified at zero. The accountant's note in the file: <i>first month the return and the ledger were the same document.</i></p><p><b>The certificate chase (Chapter 4).</b> The WHT Receivable history is rebuilt from allocation records: <b>₦1.34m of deductions suffered over two years — certificates held for only ₦610k</b>. The chase is instituted as a standing worklist: outstanding certificates per customer, aged, worked like receivables — corporate finance teams contacted with the professional ask, the government customer escalated through its due process. Eight months later, certificates for a further <b>₦590k</b> are recovered and handed to the accountant for the CIT credit — the business's own tax, un-paid-twice. The remaining <b>₦140k</b>, deductions by two defunct customers, is written to the accountant's judgment with its file: even the loss is a documented decision.</p><p><b>The reserve's first test (Chapter 7).</b> Month four, a heavy import quarter squeezes cash, and the old instinct surfaces in a management meeting: <i>the VAT reserve has ₦2.1m sitting idle — borrow it for the Emzor payment, replace it by the 21st.</i> The custody rule is restated — the most expensive credit line in Nigeria, taken by default — and the alternative is worked instead: module 4's routine accelerates two collections, and a slow stock line is released. The reserve stays whole; remittance day passes as a funded transfer. By Q3 the reserve sweep is the month's first standing Internal Transfer rather than its last argument.</p><p><b>The desk query (Chapter 8).</b> Month nine brings the authority's automated mismatch letter — for the <b>pre-system</b> period, where a parallel spreadsheet had filed what the old records could not reproduce. The reply is built on record: the current months demonstrated folder by folder (register, bridge, return, remittance entry, zero check), the legacy divergence quantified and explained, the accountant leading the position. The query closes with a small legacy settlement and one sentence in the closing letter worth keeping: <i>current periods reconcile to source.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 10 — Common mistakes & the fifth law", "11", "<p>The tax layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — custody spent.</b> Symptom: remittance day as a recurring crisis; the VAT balance funded by scramble; penalties and interest as standing line items. Cause: the state's money used as float (Chapters 1, 7). Fix: the reserve account with its scheduled Internal Transfers, the cash-strain problem solved with real tools, and the custody rule taught until it holds.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the parallel computation.</b> Symptom: returns prepared in spreadsheets beside the system; filed and booked numbers diverging; nobody able to say which is true. Cause: two sources (Chapter 6). Fix: the return from the register extract, the bridges as the pre-filing gate, and the spreadsheet demoted to a formatting tool.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the unreconciled registers.</b> Symptom: register totals and account movements drifting apart unexamined; the desk review's mismatch letter arriving as a surprise. Cause: the bridges skipped (Chapters 3, 6) — while the authority's e-invoicing rails run their half of the comparison automatically. Fix: the bridge as a standing monthly line, divergences worked before filing, the zero check closing every cycle.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — certificates uncollected.</b> Symptom: years of WHT suffered with a certificate file that fits in an envelope; the CIT bill paid gross. Cause: the sufferer side run on resignation (Chapter 4). Fix: the deduction captured in the Payment Entry's Deductions table at allocation, the chase as a standing aged worklist, and the recovered certificates handed to the accountant with their evidence — the module's most reliably self-funding routine.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — payroll beside the system.</b> Symptom: salaries in a spreadsheet, one lump journal a month, PAYE computed elsewhere, state splits improvised, the annual return a reconstruction project. Cause: a register that can produce any slice replaced by one that produces none (Chapter 5). Fix: payroll into the system or rigorously bridged, the tie chain checked monthly, payslips standing, the annual return reduced to an extract.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — penalties as a cost of doing business.</b> Symptom: late-filing and late-remittance penalties absorbed without comment, month after month. Cause: no calendar, no owners, no reserve — the module absent. Fix: the calendar with named owners and reminders, the reserve funded, and the penalty line watched to zero.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: create the typed custody accounts and state the custody rule; configure the Sales and Purchase Taxes and Charges Templates and Item Tax Templates for a mixed-supply business; run both VAT bridges from the register reports to the General Ledger; set up a Tax Withholding Category on a supplier and record a customer's deduction in the Payment Entry Deductions table; state the payroll tie chain and the payslip rule; run the six-step routine and read the zero check's four stories; build the calendar and the reserve with its Internal Transfer sweeps; and state the three authority encounter types with the evidence posture.</p><p><b>The fifth law: tax money is held, not owned — reconciled to its registers, filed from the system, remitted from the reserve, and zeroed every cycle.</b> Module 6 covers cost centres and dimensions.</p>"),
]

